# Resonance-Delay Techno

Ingredients: Hardanger fiddle + dub techno + prepared Rhodes + gran cassa + bass flute.

Creative Catalyst: A fiddle player and electronic producer discover that the natural sympathetic resonance after a bowed note can hand the phrase to a delayed electronic chord.

Genre Description: Resonance-Delay Techno runs at 112–120 BPM with compact four-on-the-floor kick, sparse percussion, short analog bass, filtered chord echoes, and large negative spaces. Hardanger fiddle remains central through open-string drones, sympathetic understrings, bowed harmonics, modal figures, and long releases. The main structural idea is BOW → WAKE → RELEASE → ECHO: direct tone, sympathetic-string bloom, bow release, then a delayed Rhodes or bass response. A second idea, Delayed Floor, places harmony slightly after the acoustic release. Sympathetic Hold sections reduce delay so real understring resonance becomes the sustained field.

## Genre DNA

Tempo & Pulse: 112–120 BPM, 4/4 dub-techno pulse with long fiddle phrases crossing barlines.

Harmonic Palette: Dorian, Aeolian, open fifths, suspended seconds, modal thirds, minor sevenths, add9 voicings, drones.

Production Signatures: Resonance Handoff; Delayed Floor; Sympathetic Hold; room microphone lifted after releases; frequency-limited dub feedback; no synthetic fiddle doubling.

Dynamics: Intensity grows through stronger bow pressure, brighter feedback, deeper gran-cassa accents, denser chord returns, and shorter gaps between handoffs.

## Scene & Origin Lore

The style forms in a retired coastal radio-repeater station above a Norwegian fjord, surrounded by transmitter racks, signal maps, battery cabinets, fogged windows, relay logs, and a distant tower light. The group learns that continuous delay blurs the acoustic instrument, while delay introduced only after bow release preserves the distinction between physical resonance and electronic memory.

## Typical Instruments / Instruments

Hardanger fiddle, close condenser, body ribbon microphone, distant room microphone, dub-techno drum machine, analog mono bass, prepared Rhodes, tape delay, gran cassa, brushed steel, bass flute, short room reverb.

For Fans Of: dub-techno spaciousness, chamber minimalism, ambient techno, bowed-string resonance, and acoustic-electronic music built around decay.

